1.4.0: compatibility hardening - engine-level parity and a frozen matrix

An audit of camera/film pairings confirmed the CLI matrix correct in
all 65 cells, and closed the one real gap: the WebAssembly engine now
enforces compatibility itself (sealed cameras and process mismatches
are refused inside the artifact, with unknown processes never blocked,
matching the CLI's treatment of out-of-library cubes), rather than
relying on the page's dropdown filtering alone. engine.js passes the
stock's process token; node tests assert the refusal and the
unknown-process allowance.

The full compatibility matrix is frozen as scripts/test_compat.sh (31
cells plus the --any-film override) and runs in make check against a
stub film library, so any future preset or resolver change that bends
the model fails the build.

Two page bugs found by reproducing a user-reported leak (a Polaroid
stock rideable onto 35mm-slide via the sample strip): clicking a sample
set the suggested camera programmatically, which fires no change event,
so the compatibility rebuild never ran and the previous camera's film
list survived; and the rebuild itself cleared only select.options,
leaving the previous optgroup elements behind. Fixed by dispatching the
change event from useSample and clearing all select children; the exact
reported flow (instant camera -> sample click -> slide camera) is now a
page regression test. The engine-level enforcement added in this
release independently backstops both: even a leaked pairing is refused
inside the wasm.
